comments[7]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-25T00:09:23.381Z","harness:codex","Intake source: 1580. Duplicate check: complete strict 3101-item all-status corpus with no omissions, request-specific searches and current open/in_progress lists; related delivered lineage retained. Report recorded for independent verification. No implementation or completion claimed by intake."
  "2026-11-01T11:09:04.697Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation: new src/core/store/settings-sections.ts. readSettingsSection(pmRoot, section, { validate, defaultValue }) returns { section, source: \"stored\" | \"default\", value, revision }. The revision is \"sha256:<hex>\" of the stable-stringified stored value, or null when the section is absent. writeSettingsSection(pmRoot, section, value | undefined, { expectedRevision, validate }) replaces or removes the section under the workspace-history lock, appends one settings:section:write event, keeps every other key, writes nothing for an unchanged value, and makes the write compare-and-set when expectedRevision is given (null requires absence). Typed refusals: settings_section_name_invalid and settings_section_reserved (the core keys come from the new CORE_SETTINGS_SECTION_KEYS, derived from the validator shape so they cannot drift), settings_section_document_malformed (reason settings_json_malformed or settings_json_not_object, instead of a thrown JSON.parse error), settings_section_invalid (validator throw, bounded message; also values JSON cannot represent), settings_section_revision_conflict (exit 4, with expected_revision and current_revision added to PmCliErrorContext), and settings_section_document_missing (exit 3). Both functions and settingsSectionRevision are public SDK exports through runtime-primitives. ExtensionCommandSdk gains readSettingsSection and writeSettingsSection bound to the invocation tracker, so a package using context.sdk inherits any future settings backend; the backend abstraction itself stays with pm-gh1399. Docs: new section in docs/SDK_CONFIGURATION_SAFETY.md. Tests: tests/unit/core/store/settings-sections.spec.ts, 16 cases, 100% branch coverage of the module.\n"
  "2026-11-01T15:40:54.738Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 review follow-up (CodeRabbit). writeSettingsSection gains an optional author, and the host-bound context.sdk.writeSettingsSection passes the invocation author, so a command run with an explicit author records it on the settings:section:write history event (it used to fall back to the workspace default and detected identity). Values JSON would store differently from what was validated now refuse with settings_section_invalid naming the first drifting path: non-finite numbers (Infinity or NaN become null), undefined array elements, function or symbol values, symbol keys and non-plain objects such as Date. Null-prototype plain objects still store exactly. Ordering note: gate_evidence was recorded after close and release because the defect-evidence gate first failed on the hosted run; it is a follow-up record, the append-only history is unchanged."
  "2026-11-01T17:31:23.661Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh). settingsSectionRevision used stableStringify, whose object accumulator drops an own __proto__ key, so two values differing only beneath such a key shared a revision and a stale compare-and-set write could pass; revisions now hash a prototype-safe canonical JSON (ordinary values keep the same revision string). readSettingsSection hashed the stored value after running the validator on it, so an in-place normalizer changed the revision a later write had to present; the revision is now computed first and the validator receives a detached clone. The JSON-drift check now rejects arrays with holes, extra or symbol keys, a subclass or a toJSON hook, and objects with a function-valued toJSON (a data key named toJSON is still stored). Writing a value with an own __proto__ key, or prototype beneath constructor, used to fail untyped in the history patcher; it now refuses with settings_section_invalid naming the key path. Specs cover each case."
  "2026-11-01T18:08:31.174Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review, second pass: the JSON-drift check ran after JSON.stringify, so a toJSON hook that deleted itself still stored an object instead of the validated array. The check now inspects without serializing (cycle-safe, rejecting accessor-backed values, toJSON hooks, holes and extra keys) before any hook or getter can run, then serializes proven plain data. Circular values and bigints refuse as not representable. Specs cover a self-removing hook, a getter and a circular value."
  "2026-11-01T18:18:54.138Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review, third pass: the drift check read toJSON through Reflect.get before inspecting descriptors, so a self-removing toJSON getter could rewrite a validated field (strict true to false) and then pass; and a non-enumerable own data property passed although JSON drops it. Descriptors are now checked first (data properties only, enumerable for objects, an array's length excepted) before any hook lookup, so no getter runs before the value is proven plain. Specs cover the self-removing getter, a non-enumerable property and a getter-backed array element."
  "2026-11-01T18:26:58.428Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1648 Codex review, fourth pass: a non-enumerable extra property on an array (for example Object.defineProperty(['a'], 'strict', { value: true })) passed the drift check because Object.keys misses it, and JSON dropped it silently. Arrays now also require that their only own property names are the dense indices plus length; a spec covers the hidden array property."
